re  fortunate to be in good health  and financially secure for me it was  just a time to really reflect at the end  of the day  you know what do i want to accomplish in  my life and to be honest  like if i didn't take care of my mom you  know nothing else would have mattered  it hasn't necessarily been easy but the  decision was  microlight global news toronto  what happens when families want to pull  their loved ones out of long-term care  homes can they return without going to  the end of the wait lists  mike drollay explores that issue  less is more still ahead a new study  finds shorter work  weeks are better for business  working less may actually be more and  the key to success according to the  world's largest study out of iceland  a four-year study involving more than 2  000 public servants looked at the impact  of  a shorter work week and it found that  working full-time  but only four days a week provides just  as much pay  and productivity as ann gaviola explains  a community in canada  is putting it to the test zorra is a  rural township just east of london  ontario it's 14 municipal staffers part  of one of the country's largest trials  of the four-day work week  they work a compressed week longer hours  from either monday to thursday or  tuesday to friday  it works out to the same number of hours  and the same pay for those that have  small children it's one less day of day  care  in my case my father's elderly so that's  my day i take him to appointments  there are challenges scheduling meetings  when all participants are working  setting voicemail and out of office  reminders after a successful pilot the  second phase of the eight-month trial  kicked off this week and runs until  october staff and researchers at western  university will evaluate from there and  make recommendations for the future  i found within even the first couple of  weeks that my work-life balance was  improved and i was able to do a lot more  things with family and in my personal  life and come to work  recharged the township hopes to use it  as a recruitment tool keeping the  community competitive with other centers  that can offer more money  zora's project is modeled on a  pioneering trial of 60 public servants  in guysborough nova scotia  when news broke that we were trying this  four day work week pilot  i had so many classmates messaging me  and asking if  zoro was hiring or if there's any jobs  going to be coming available because  everyone was very interested  but labor experts say the four-day week  won't work for certain types of jobs  painstaking physical labor or tasks that  require intense concentration like  developing computer code  see reduced productivity with longer  shifts the pandemic has been a taste of  the impact of compressed staggered  schedules on a larger scale  unintended consequences some good some  bad well i mean look at traffic  right look at traffic i mean the  pandemic has shown us that  you get the cars off the road you can  get to where you're going pretty quickly  i've taken  calls from all across the country from  municipalities and private businesses  also looking for information on the four  day work week so it seems to be catching  on  and gaviola global news toronto  next the 22 year old canadian winning  hearts at wimbledon  and fighting the world's best for a  trophy  a canadian tennis player pulled off a  thrilling win at wimbledon  dennis shapovalov advanced to his first  ever  grand slam semi-final he beat russia's  karen hachenov in five sets  chapavalov's next match is against  number one in the  world novak djokovic this friday and it  certainly will not be easy  but as crystal gomezing reports the  underdog canadian  is ready for a fight 22 and  already a fan favorite at wimbledon  denis chapel vallov  the canadian from richmond hill  victorious  he's headed to his first grand slam  semi-final  but to get there it took extraordinary  resolve  pushing through a marathon thriller that  lasted nearly  three and a half hours if he wants to be  the first canadian  ever to win wimbledon he'll first have  to take down  a tennis giant novak djokovic  the defending champion and a legend on  grass  i think anything is possible and when  when you look at the scoreboard you know  for  first thing on whatever it is friday  it's going to be 0-0 you know so  that's it want proof anything is  possible  two rising canadian stars made it to  these quarter-finals  felix o'jay aliasim lost his match but  his performance at wimbledon  also establishes new career heights at  20.  i think the the thing that's most  surprising about dennis and felix is  actually their age because  players right now peak in their late 20s  and you know to think felix isn't going  to be 21 until august and dennis  just turned 22 like their best years are  actually ahead of them  the two are good friends and have risen  through the ranks together  in 2014 o'jay aliya sim won the junior  grand slam at the u.s  open shapovalov won the junior grand  slam at wimbledon  fast forward to the main event this week  and it's clear the future of canadian  men's tennis  is now there's going to be more kids  that not only enter the game  because of their success but also  believe in their game when they're 11 12  13 years of age on the men's side only  milos raonic  has ever made it to the wimbledon final  it's a tennis match and it could go  either way and i have full belief  in in me and my team right now shepa  vetloff has a chance to make history  every time he takes the court crystal  gomansing global news  london and that's global national for  this wednesday  i'm farah nasser there is more sports  history to be made tonight  as the montreal canadiens and tampa bay  lightning face off for game five of the  stanley cup finals  and here's hoping these hockey moves  will help the haves  canadian fungus dog pander is sending  good luck  through dance until tomorrow take care  of yourselves  and each oth